
A Morloc module can be directly compiled into a CLI tool where exported
functions are transformed into typed subcommands. Hints from special comments
and type-level information direct the generation of the CLI program and its
usage statements.

Here is a minimal example:

[source, morloc]
----
import root-py

source Py from "main.py" ("foo", "bar")

--' Take two reals and do thing
foo :: Real -> Real -> Real

--' Convert a list of reals into a thing
bar :: [Real] -> Real
----

The special comment `--'` introduces a docstring that is attached to the
following type signature and will be propagated through to the code generated by
the backend.

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o nexus main.loc
$ ./nexus -h
----

The generated help lists the provided documentation along with the available commands and options.

Each exported function becomes a command:

----
Commands:
  foo  Take two reals and do thing
  bar  Convert a list of reals into a thing
----

More detailed information about each exported subcommand may be accessed as well:

[source, console]
----
$ ./nexus foo -h
Take two reals and do thing

Usage: nexus <nexus_options> foo <command_options>

General Options:
  -h, --help  Print help (see more with '--help')

Positional arguments:
  1:  type: Real
  2:  type: Real

Return: Real
----

Note that the `Usage` line above separates nexus options from command
options. All Morloc CLI arguments are partitioned into two zones. The *nexus
zone* holds options that are common to all Morloc CLIs. The *command zone* holds
all options that are particular to the program. The same flag may be present in
both zones and they will not conflict.

The nexus zone has no positionals, so ALL nexus arguments are formed as
`-x/--option` arguments that may take a fixed number of values. The nexus and
command zones are separated by either the `@` token or a subcommand name. The `@`
token is required only in single export programs where the subcommand name is
optional.

To pass a nexus option in single-command mode, place it left of `@`:

[source, console]
----
$ ./hw -f jsonl @ -n Weena  # Set output format to JSONL
"Weena"
----

Here are a few more examples:

[source, console]
----
$ ./hw                     # sole export runs with no args
$ ./hw -h                  # print help for this program
$ ./hw -h @                # print help for the nexus
$ ./hw -n Weena            # user flag reaches the command zone directly
$ ./hw hello Weena         # use the optional subcommand name
$ ./hw @ -n Weena          # explicit @ separator, empty nexus zone
$ ./hw -f mpk @ -n Weena   # set the output format to messagepack
----

For a program with two or more exports, argv looks like:

[source, console]
----
$ ./foo -h                 # get help for the program
$ ./foo bar -h             # get help for one subcommand
$ ./foo -h @               # get help for the nexus
$ ./foo -p baz 5           # run one command with no nexus args
$ ./foo -f jsonl baz 5     # subcommand name separates zones
$ ./foo @ baz 5            # explicit @ may be used as well
----

The subcommand tokens `bar` and `baz` above mark the zone boundary and
belong to the command zone.

Morloc CLIs are deeply interwoven with the Morloc type system and runtime. They
inherit Morloc's formatting, compression support, and a rich set of type-driven
features. We will cover these in the following sections.

